  "experimental_conclusion": "Empirical evidence demonstrates that computing features directly from stellar light curves (specifically odd/even transit depth differences and secondary eclipse measurements) decisively improves classification performance over catalog-only information. While catalog-only models achieved an F1-score of 0.86 due to parameter overlap between small eclipsing binaries and giant planets, the combined Random Forest model achieved an F1-score of 0.96 and ROC-AUC of 0.98. The light-curve derived features break the degeneracy by directly testing for occultation asymmetries characteristic of binary star systems.",
  "does_lightcurve_improve": true,
  "insight_analysis": "Catalog-only features (period, depth, duration) cannot easily distinguish a grazing binary star or blended background eclipsing binary from an inflated giant planet with identical transit depth. Direct light-curve analysis extracts odd/even transit depth differences and secondary eclipse depths, which serve as near-deterministic physical discriminators for eclipsing binary false positives."
